entry.summary:
Somebody types "can we loop in finance on this?" in a product channel. Nobody That is not a tooling problem in any obvious sense. Slack worked exactly as The instinct is to grep for the word "finance" and alert on it. That produces What actually carries the signal is structure. A Slack message is not plain Can we loop in <#C01ABCDEF|finance> before this goes out?
That is a channel reference, distinct from a mention that notifies the So the bot parses references rather than words. It maps channel IDs to what
It joins every public channel by itself. The obvious build asks an admin channel_created It runs on Socket Mode. The usual Slack integration takes an inbound Socket Mode has a consequence that only shows up on deploy. Because the bot The fix is a sidecar: a minimal HTTP server inside the same process whose only import express from "express";
const app = express(); app.get("/", (_req, res) => res.send("Bot is running")); app.listen(process.env.PORT || 3000);
Ten lines that do nothing for the product and without which the product does The bot holds channels:join, channels:history and chat:write. Public Coverage went from whichever channels someone remembered to whichever channels The wider lesson is about where automation should sit. This one requires no configuration, adapts to new channels on its own, and nobody in the workspace has to change how they write. The best version of a system like this is one the team never thinks about, which is also the version that is hardest to justify building, because the evidence of it working is the absence of an expensive surprise.
